# Operating Local Trust: Bundles, Identity, And The Vault

[![Run in Livebook](https://livebook.dev/badge/v1/blue.svg)](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Frefpath%2Fsigil_guard%2Fblob%2Fmain%2Fnotebooks%2Ftrust-bundles-identity-and-vault.livemd)

Runtime policy is only as trustworthy as the material it consumes. A pattern
set fetched ad hoc from the network, an actor label supplied by a model, or a
provider key copied into tool arguments can undermine an otherwise careful
gate.

This chapter operates the local side of that problem: a signed offline trust
bundle, per-boot rollback state, audit-safe quarantine, host-resolved identity,
and an encrypted in-memory vault. SigilGuard intentionally performs no trust
bundle HTTP fetch. The host owns transport and passes bytes into verification.

## Setup

```elixir
repo_root = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)
local_checkout? = File.regular?(Path.join(repo_root, "mix.exs"))

{sigil_guard_dep, install_opts} =
  if local_checkout? do
    {
      {:sigil_guard, path: repo_root, env: :prod},
      [
        config_path: Path.join(repo_root, "config/config.exs"),
        lockfile: Path.join(repo_root, "mix.lock")
      ]
    }
  else
    {{:sigil_guard, "~> 1.0"}, []}
  end

Mix.install([sigil_guard_dep], install_opts)
```

## Build A Deterministic Development Bundle

`dev_bundle/1` exists for development and tests. It produces a real signed
DSSE bundle, marks its provenance as `dev`, and is intentionally not a
substitute for production key ceremony. The fixed seed and clock make this
tutorial repeatable.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.TrustBundle
alias SigilGuard.TrustBundle.{Cache, Quarantine}

Cache.clear()
Quarantine.clear()

bundle_now = ~U[2026-08-19 10:00:00.000Z]
bundle_seed = :crypto.hash(:sha256, "sigil-guard-livebook-dev-bundle")

custom_patterns = [
  %{
    "set" => "secret",
    "name" => "conference_badge",
    "regex" => "BADGE-[A-Z0-9]{8}",
    "severity" => "high",
    "replacement_hint" => "[CONFERENCE_BADGE]",
    "max_match_bytes" => 32
  }
]

{:ok, bundle} =
  TrustBundle.dev_bundle(
    seed: bundle_seed,
    now: bundle_now,
    ttl_ms: 3_600_000,
    patterns: custom_patterns,
    policies: [%{"id" => "talk-policy", "digest" => String.duplicate("a", 64)}],
    identity_issuers: ["spiffe://conference"],
    cache: true
  )

true = bundle.dev?
1 = bundle.sequence
1 = bundle.root_version
{:ok, ^bundle} = Cache.get(bundle.bundle_id)

%{
  bundle_id: bundle.bundle_id,
  digest: bundle.digest,
  sequence: bundle.sequence,
  root_version: bundle.root_version,
  development_only?: bundle.dev?,
  provenance: bundle.document["provenance"]
}
```

The verified bundle can be passed through an in-memory map, JSON bytes, a local
file, or an application `priv/` resource. Core verification opens no network
connection. Here we round-trip its envelope as bytes without touching disk.

```elixir
encoded_envelope = Jason.encode!(bundle.envelope)

{:ok, loaded_from_bytes} =
  TrustBundle.load({:binary, encoded_envelope},
    now: bundle_now,
    cache: false,
    quarantine: true
  )

true = loaded_from_bytes.digest == bundle.digest
%{source: elem(loaded_from_bytes.source, 0), digest_stable?: true}
```

## Resolve Pattern Sets Deliberately

Each of the three sets — secrets, injection, and poisoning — is independently
replaceable. Providing a custom secret set replaces that set; absent injection
and poisoning sets keep their built-in defaults. This avoids an update to one
class accidentally disabling the others.

```elixir
{:ok, pattern_sets} = TrustBundle.pattern_sets(bundle)

[conference_pattern] = pattern_sets.secret
true = length(pattern_sets.injection) > 0
true = length(pattern_sets.poisoning) > 0

{:hit, [badge_hit]} =
  SigilGuard.scan("check in with BADGE-AB12CD34", patterns: pattern_sets.secret)

"conference_badge" = badge_hit.name

redacted_badge =
  SigilGuard.scan_and_redact(
    "check in with BADGE-AB12CD34",
    patterns: pattern_sets.secret
  )

false = String.contains?(redacted_badge, "BADGE-AB12CD34")

%{
  custom_secret_pattern: conference_pattern.name,
  injection_defaults_retained: length(pattern_sets.injection),
  poisoning_defaults_retained: length(pattern_sets.poisoning),
  output: redacted_badge
}
```

## Cache Floors Resist Rollback

The cache is per boot. It stores only verified snapshots and remembers the
highest accepted sequence/root floor for each bundle id. Re-inserting the exact
same verified snapshot is idempotent. A fabricated conflicting snapshot is
shown only to make the cache contract visible; normal callers reach
`Cache.put/1` only after signature verification.

```elixir
{:ok, ^bundle} = Cache.put(bundle)

conflicting_snapshot = %{
  bundle
  | digest: String.duplicate("0", 64)
}

{:error, :sequence_below_floor} = Cache.put(conflicting_snapshot)
1 = Cache.floor(bundle.bundle_id)

%{accepted_floor: Cache.floor(bundle.bundle_id), rollback: :rejected}
```

## Failed Material Goes To Audit-Safe Quarantine

Quarantine keeps a reason and bounded evidence metadata, never the trust
bundle payload or signature bytes. That makes it useful for operations without
turning the diagnostic table into a second sensitive store.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid_envelope} =
  TrustBundle.load({:map, %{"payload" => "not-a-valid-envelope"}},
    cache: false,
    quarantine: true
  )

[quarantine_record | _] = Enum.reverse(Quarantine.list())

false = Map.has_key?(quarantine_record, :payload)
false = Map.has_key?(quarantine_record, :signature)

%{
  reason: quarantine_record.reason,
  recorded_fields: quarantine_record |> Map.keys() |> Enum.sort(),
  raw_material_retained?: false
}
```

## Identity Is Host Truth

For simple deployments, `Identity.Static` resolves an actor through an ordered,
validated prefix table. A full deployment can implement the same behaviour
from OIDC, SPIFFE, a session, or hardware-backed identity. The model does not
get to announce its own trust level.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.Identity
alias SigilGuard.Identity.Static

previous_mappings = Application.get_env(:sigil_guard, :trust_mappings)

Application.put_env(:sigil_guard, :trust_mappings, [
  {"spiffe://conference/operators/*", :high},
  {"user:*", :medium}
])

:high = Static.trust_level("spiffe://conference/operators/alice")
:medium = Static.trust_level("user:bob")
:low = Static.trust_level("model:self-asserted-admin")
true = Identity.sufficient_trust?(:high, :medium)
false = Identity.sufficient_trust?(:low, :medium)

identity_summary = %{
  operator: Static.trust_level("spiffe://conference/operators/alice"),
  user: Static.trust_level("user:bob"),
  unknown: Static.trust_level("model:self-asserted-admin"),
  hierarchy: Identity.trust_levels()
}

if is_nil(previous_mappings) do
  Application.delete_env(:sigil_guard, :trust_mappings)
else
  Application.put_env(:sigil_guard, :trust_mappings, previous_mappings)
end

identity_summary
```

## Keep Secret Values Out Of Tool Arguments

The built-in vault is an ETS-backed development backend. It encrypts each
entry with AES-256-GCM under a 32-byte master key and returns an opaque
reference. Entries are process-local and disappear on restart; durable
deployments implement the `SigilGuard.Vault` behaviour against their KMS or
secret store.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.Vault
alias SigilGuard.Vault.InMemory

vault_pid =
  case Process.whereis(InMemory) do
    nil ->
      {:ok, pid} =
        InMemory.start_link(master_key: :crypto.hash(:sha256, "livebook-vault-key"))

      pid

    pid ->
      pid
  end

true = Process.alive?(vault_pid)

provider_secret = "synthetic-provider-key-for-livebook"
{:ok, vault_id} = Vault.encrypt(provider_secret, "Conference provider credential", InMemory)

true = Vault.exists?(vault_id, InMemory)
false = String.contains?(vault_id, provider_secret)
true = {vault_id, "Conference provider credential"} in InMemory.list_entries()

%{
  reference: vault_id,
  description: "Conference provider credential",
  plaintext_in_reference?: String.contains?(vault_id, provider_secret)
}
```

Only the narrow host callback that needs the credential should resolve it, at
the final moment. Do not give a model a generic vault lookup tool.

```elixir
{:ok, resolved_secret} = Vault.decrypt(vault_id, InMemory)
true = resolved_secret == provider_secret

:ok = InMemory.delete(vault_id)
false = Vault.exists?(vault_id, InMemory)
{:error, :not_found} = Vault.decrypt(vault_id, InMemory)

%{resolved_inside_host_callback?: true, deleted_after_use?: true}
```

## Takeaway

Trust bundles bring reviewed policy material to the process without giving core
a network client. Identity comes from the host, not the prompt. Secrets remain
opaque until a narrow callback needs them. Together those choices prevent the
security plane from becoming another source of ambient authority.
